---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="From code to pixels"
  description="The path a sprite takes from a loaded asset blob to actually appearing on the screen."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'How the Game Works', href: `${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/` },
    { label: 'Sprite pipeline' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The DS draws sprites by reading two pieces of hardware memory: a
    <strong>tile data</strong> region in VRAM, and an <strong>OAM</strong>
    region with a list of sprite descriptors. Each descriptor says
    "show the chip at this position, at this size, using this palette."
    The game's job is to keep those two regions filled in.
  </p>

  <h2>Shadow OAM</h2>
  <p>
    The hardware OAM only updates safely during vertical blank — the
    brief gap between drawing one frame and the next. So the game
    keeps a copy of the OAM in main RAM (the "shadow OAM"), edits the
    copy whenever a sprite changes, and at vertical blank it does a
    single DMA copy from the shadow to the hardware. Same idea for
    tile data in VRAM.
  </p>

  <p>
    Four wrapper functions handle the transfers:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Shadow OAM main → hardware OAM (top screen)</li>
    <li>Shadow OAM sub → hardware OAM (bottom screen)</li>
    <li>Shadow tile data → OBJ VRAM main</li>
    <li>Shadow tile data → OBJ VRAM sub</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    All four are called from exactly two places: the ARM9's main
    vertical blank handler, and the rendering engine overlay's
    vertical blank handler. So sprite changes always show up in sync
    with the screen refresh.
  </p>

  <h2>Sprite constructors</h2>
  <p>
    Game code doesn't poke OAM directly. It calls a "sprite constructor"
    function with nine arguments — which sprite slot to use, and the
    IDs of the tile sheet, palette, sprite definition, and tilemap to
    pull from the content cache. The constructor sets up the entry in
    the shadow OAM and registers it in a sprite-slot array.
  </p>

  <p>
    There's a primary constructor and an alternate. They differ mostly
    in which cache table they consult. Both ultimately write to the
    same shadow OAM.
  </p>

  <h2>Attribute updaters</h2>
  <p>
    Once a sprite is placed, the game can change it without rebuilding
    it. Helper functions update the position, the palette bank, the
    render mode flags. These all write to the slot in the sprite-slot
    array, which gets flushed at the next vertical blank.
  </p>

  <p>
    A sprite slot record holds the sprite's current state — its OAM
    attributes, its size, its current cell, animation state if any.
    Updates touch specific fields and leave the rest alone, so a
    moving sprite doesn't have to re-specify its palette every frame.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the same sprite chip can appear many times</h2>
  <p>
    Because OAM entries are independent. A single tile sheet can hold
    one tile of "fire particle," and OAM can have 30 different OAM
    entries all pointing at that tile, each at a different position
    and rotation. So one chip in VRAM can produce 30 visible sprites
    on screen. The buyitem screen exploits this: a single "decorative
    star" tile in the tile sheet shows up at six different positions
    via six OAM entries.
  </p>

  <p>
    When we extract sprite chips to PNGs, we get the tile-sheet view —
    each unique chip once. When the game renders, the chips are
    multiplied out. That's part of why an extracted sprite gallery
    can look "missing parts" compared to a screenshot.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
